it's quite a strange feeling here  because the world has paid all this  attention to the country people dismiss  often some of the news that's coming in  about looming invasion it's something  that they've learned to live with for so  long but underneath once you scratch the  surface people open up and you see there  really is a genuine concern the question  that everybody is asking is will russia  invade my name is nils adler and i'm a  freelance journalist for al jazeera in  ukraine and between us i move from  cautious optimism to extreme pessimism  on whether russia will invade ukraine  i think what's important to remember  about this conflict is it didn't begin  as conventional warfare the ukrainian  army was in a terrible state in 2014  when the conflict began and so it was  volunteers people who had no experience  of fighting before and had to just pick  up a weapon and learn how to fight  the soldiers fighting right now this  trench warfare it's not the modern age  warfare we imagine with drones and  high-tech gadgets this is just soldiers  dug into a trench and they're just  fighting facing sniper fire and  artillery fight  i spoke to a soldier vladimir this was a  tough guy a tough soldier he began  slowly opening up talking a little bit  more about tactics and warfare but  slowly as we spoke i discovered he had  suffered this traumatic brain injury  twice he started to admit that maybe  there was a mental health aspect to this  i said  what do you do with all these feelings  and then he just pointed at his chest  and said i hold it all in here i tried  to get in contact with him again but i  was told he's not in a good place he's  really struggling to adapt  another forgotten aspect about this  conflict is the children in pulpasma  there was a young girl she was nine  years old for carolina she remembers the  shelling back in 2014 taking shelter in  this kind of makeshift bomb shelter that  the family had created and she is scared  that it's going to happen again as a  father of three and myself when i was  speaking to carolina i thought this is  not something children should have to be  thinking about  so i visited karaoke which is located  precariously close to the border with  russia now this is an incredibly  important city for ukraine both  economically but also because it's a  technological hub i met one  i.t specialist he was a avid  motorcyclist but when he realized that  there was an increased potential for an  invasion he sold his bike and he bought  a gun and now he works with his computer  but by his side is his gun and his body  armor and he's ready to go he says in  five minutes he would be ready to defend  his city  was mentioned by vladimir zelinski the  president of ukraine as a potential  first target for the russians should  they invade  [Music]  the ukrainian army is in a much better  state than it was back in 2014 and 2015.  but  this is russia we're talking about  [Music]  so civilians have really taken it upon  themselves to defend their city this is  not just about repelling an invasion of  their city but also keeping up a  resistance movement if they were  occupied  so right now russian troops have amassed  around ukraine's borders the americans  are saying innovation is imminent the  ukrainian president has asked for calm  the soldiers i spoke to on the front  line they're just focused on the same  thing and that's the separatists on the  other side in the other trenches  when they talk about the current  tensions they understand there's been an  increase in tensions but they can't  afford to be distracted by this that for  them is another world it's a world for  politicians to deal with for them  right now it's just about the trenches  it's about the muddy slog the everyday  slog of having to fight and face these  threats  [Music]  [Applause]
 

